  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Above normal temperatures and increasing chances\nof showers for the end of the week. \n\nHigh pressure offshore will continue to pump milder and increasingly\nmore humid air across the area today and Friday. High temps will\nbe above normal both days with much above normal on Friday. Except\nfor a few morning sprinkles across the far N/W this morning, rains\nare not expected today or tonight. \n\nThis will change for Friday as a cold front will pass across the\nregion late Friday and Friday night and will settle over Delmarva\non Saturday. Scattered showers will develop Friday afternoon, \nmainly north and west of the I-95 corridor, then scattered showers\nspread east Friday night. The showers taper off in most areas by\nSaturday morning, but across southern Delaware and south NJ, we'll\nkeep some clouds and scattered showers for Saturday as the front\nbecomes nearly stationary over the Mid- Atlantic. Overall, QPF \nwill be less than 1/10 inch but a few areas closer to 2/10ths of\nan inch possible. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled conditions are expected for the later\npart of the weekend. Chances for rain on Sunday have increased.\n\nHigh pressure then builds into the region from the north on Saturday.\nMostly sunny skies for most of the area and partly to mostly cloudy\nskies across Delmarva and extreme southern New Jersey with seasonable\ntemperatures with highs in the upper 60s to around 70. These temperatures\nare near normal for early October. \n\nAfter that, low pressure develops on that stalled front and\npasses cross the Mid-Atlantic and Delmarva Sunday and Sunday \nnight. The overnight models continue the previous trend of \nbringing higher POPs further north for Sunday. The present fcst \nnow has low-end likely POPS for southern Delaware and the 50% \nchance line runs along the Delaware valley and up into Monmouth\nCounty. Low chance pops are included now for the southern \nPoconos and Lehigh Valley\n\nHigh pressure then builds into the region for the start of the \nnew work week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Tidal flooding continues to recede in the back bays of New \nJersey and Delaware.\n\nTidal levels continue to decline and recede as offshore flow continues\nto allow the back bays to drain. Some of the back bays of Barnegat\nBay, Rehoboth Bay, Jefferson Creek and Little Assawoman Bay may\nsee some spotty minor tidal flooding continue today, as winds turn\nmore shore parallel and slow the rate of drainage. However, the\nbulk of the threat is over. As a result, no coastal flooding headlines\nare currently in effect.\n\nNo further tidal flooding is expected elsewhere.",
